The relationship between the inputs to the $m^{th}$ layer ($x_{m}$) and $m+1^{th}$ layer ($x_{m+1}$) are described by the recursions 
\begin{equation} 
y_{m}(i) = \sum\limits_{j=1}^{j=N}{\bf{W}}_{m}(i,j)x_{m}(j) = \sum\limits_{j=1}^{j=N} p_{ij} \enum{in1}
\end{equation} 
and 
\begin{equation} 
x_{m+1}(i) = \mbox{g}(y_{m}(i)). \enum{in2} 
\end{equation}

Using the recursions in (\ref {eq:in1}) and (\ref {eq:in2}), $x_{m}(k)$ can be expressed as some non-linear function of the weights in the first $m-1$ layers, and the inputs to the first layer. Since the weights in the $m^{th}$ layer are independent of the inputs to the first layer and the weights in all other layers, the weights in the $m^{th}$ layer will also be independent of any non-linear function of these quantities. Therefore, $W_{m}(i,j)$ is independent of $x_{m}(k)$ for all values of $i, j$ and $k$. Furthermore, since $W_{m}(i,j)$ is independent of $x_{m}(k)$ and $x_{m}(l)$, $W_{m}(i,j)$ will also be independent of $x_{m}(k)x_{m}(l)$\qedsymbol
\linebreak
\newline
Taking expectations in (\ref {eq:in1}) and using proposition 1, along with the fact that $E({\bf{W}}_{m}(i,j)) = 0$ yields 
\begin{equation} 
r_{m} = E(y_{m}(i)) = \sum\limits_{j=1}^{j=N} E({\bf{W}}_{m}(i,j))E(x_{m}(j)) = 0. \enum{in201}
\end{equation} 
Therefore,  $u_{m}^{2} = Var(y_{m}(i)) = E\left(y_{m}(i)^{2}\right) -  \left(E(y_{m}(i))\right)^{2} = E\left(y_{m}(i)^{2}\right)$. Using (\ref {eq:in1}),




\begin{equation} 
\begin{split}
u_{m}^{2} &= E\left(\sum\limits_{j=1}^{j=N}{\bf{W}}_{m}(i,j)x_{m}(j)\right)^{2} \\
&= \sum\limits_{j=1}^{j=N} E\left({\bf{W}}_{m}(i,j)^{2}x_{m}(j)^{2}\right)  + 2\sum E\left({\bf{W}}_{m}(i,j)x_{m}(j){\bf{W}}_{m}(k,l)x_{m}(l)\right). \\ \enum{in203}
\end{split}
\end{equation} 
From proposition 1, ${\bf{W}}_{m}(i,j)$ and ${\bf{W}}_{m}(k,l)$ will (a) be independent of each other, and (b) be independent of $x_{m}(j)x_{m}(l)$. Using these results, along with the fact that $E({\bf{W}}_{m}(i,j)) = 0 $ gives 
\newline
\begin{equation} 
E\left({\bf{W}}_{m}(i,j)x_{m}(j){\bf{W}}_{m}(k,l)x_{m}(l)\right) = E({\bf{W}}_{m}(i,j))E({\bf{W}}_{m}(k,l))E(x_{m}(j)x_{m}(l)) = 0. \enum{in2041}
\end{equation}
\newline
Plugging (\ref {eq:in2041}) into (\ref {eq:in203}) gives



\begin{equation} 
\begin{split}
u_{m}^{2} &= \sum\limits_{j=1}^{j=N} E\left({\bf{W}}_{m}(i,j)^{2}x_{m}(j)^{2}\right) \\ 
&= \sum\limits_{j=1}^{j=N} E\left({\bf{W}}_{m}(i,j)^{2}\right)E\left(x_{m}(j)^{2}\right) \\  
&= Nv^{2}(s_{m}^{2} + \mu_{m}^{2}) \\ \enum{in204}
\end{split}
\end{equation}
for all $i$.

To explain their finding, I begin by noting that in  \cite{glorot2010understanding}, the authors initialize the weights using samples from a uniform distribution $\left(U[-1/\sqrt{N},1/\sqrt{N}]\right)$ having a variance of  $1/3N$. Therefore, from (\ref {eq:in2061}) and (\ref {eq:in207}), with $g(0) = 0$ and $g'(0) = 1$, we have $\mu_{m} = 0$ and






\begin{equation} 
s_{m+1}^{2} = \frac{1}{3}s_{m}^{2} < s_{m}^{2}
\end{equation} \enum{seq1}
respectively. From (\ref {eq:in204}), this implies that $u_{m+1}^{2}<u_{m}^{2}$ for all $m$ (i.e., $u_{m}^{2}$ is a decreasing function of $m$).  Furthermore from proposition 2, $y_{m}(i) \sim {\cal{N}}(0,u_{m}^{2})$. Therefore, $x_{m+1}(i)$ will be the tanh transformation of a normal random variable. Using results from \cite{godfrey2009tanh}, $x_{m+1}(i) = \mbox{tanh}(y_{m}(i))$ will have a probability density function (pdf) given by 

\begin{equation} 
f(y) = \frac{1}{1-y^{2}}\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi u_{m}^{2}}}\mbox{e}^{\left({-\frac{t_{y}^{2}}{2u_{m}^{2}}}\right)} \enum{tanh1},
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{equation} 
t_{y} =  \frac{1}{2} ln\left(\frac{1+y}{1-y}\right).
\end{equation}
Plots of this pdf for different values of $u_{m}$  (provided in Figure \ref{fig:tanh}) produce trends similar to those observed by the simulation studies of \cite{glorot2010understanding} (figure 4 in their paper). A comparison of Figure \ref{fig:tanh} and figure 4 of \cite{glorot2010understanding}  suggests that $u_{m}^{2}$ is a decreasing function of $m$, as is expected.
